<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sun Tzu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 500 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sunzi, Sun Wu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sun Tzu (Sunzi, Sun Wu) is the traditional author of The Art of War, the most famous Chinese work on military strategy. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> he was a general from Qi who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">King Helü of Wu</a> around 500 BCE, though many historians doubt this and date the text to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. The book stresses deception, intelligence and winning without fighting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sun-tzu.jpg" alt="Sun Tzu" width="276" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%90%B4%E5%8F%B8%E9%A9%AC%E5%AD%99%E6%AD%A6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sun Tzu means Master Sun and is an honorific. His birth name is said to have been Sun Wu. The earliest account of his life is a short biography in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, the Records of the Grand Historian, written around 97 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>. It says he was born in Qi, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, near the end of the Spring and Autumn period. The scholar Victor Mair considers the biography essentially fictional.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-helu-of-wu">King Helü of Wu</a> heard of Sun Wu's Art of War, summoned him and asked him to show how he trained soldiers, using the king's harem of 180 concubines. Sun Tzu appointed the two concubines the king favoured most as commanders and gave orders, which they ignored, laughing. Over the king's protests he had the two commanders executed, after which the rest obeyed at once, and the king made Sun Wu a general. He is said to have led Wu to victory over the much larger state of Chu at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju">Battle of Boju</a> in 506 BCE.</p><p>Beginning in the 12th century scholars began to doubt that he existed. Ye Shi noticed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zuo-zhuan">Zuo Zhuan</a>, which describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boju">Battle of Boju</a> in far more detail and was written centuries before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, never mentions Sun Tzu, and the name Sun Wu appears in no text earlier than the Shiji.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Art of War is a Classical Chinese text in 13 chapters, each devoted to a different set of skills related to warfare, strategy and tactics. It also advocates diplomacy and the cultivation of relations with other states. The earliest parts probably date from at least a century after Sun Tzu, and since the 12th century most scholars have placed the text at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Bamboo slips found at Yinque Shan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a> in 1972 hold about a third of the chapters of the modern text, with closely matching wording.</p><p>The earliest known commentary was written by the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> in the early 3rd century CE. Emperor Shenzong of Song formalized the Seven Military Classics in 1080, and the Art of War led that anthology for almost 1,500 years. Lionel Giles completed the first annotated English translation in 1910.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sun Tzu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qi is associated with famous military thinkers: <b>Sun Wu</b>, the traditional author of The Art of War, was said to come from Qi, and his descendant Sun Bin, as strategist for the general Tian Ji, defeated the State of Wei at the Battle of Guiling in 354 or…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundations are the Five Classics, among them the Book of Songs, the oldest collection of Chinese poetry, and the Book of Changes, and the works of the philosophers, among them Confucius, Mencius, Laozi, Zhuangzi, Mozi and Han Feizi, and the Art of War of <b>Sunzi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/classical-chinese">Classical Chinese · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>With the advisers Wu Zixu and, by tradition, <b>Sun Tzu</b>, King Helü captured the Chu capital Ying in 506 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wu">State of Wu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Qi. Died in Gusu. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;All warfare is based on deception.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 1 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Hence to fight and conquer in all your battles is not supreme excellence; supreme excellence consists in breaking the enemy’s resistance without fighting.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 3 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If you know the enemy and know yourself, you need not fear the result of a hundred battles. If you know yourself but not the enemy, for every victory gained you will also suffer a defeat.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 3 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There is no instance of a country having benefited from prolonged warfare.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 2 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Water shapes its course according to the nature of the ground over which it flows; the soldier works out his victory in relation to the foe whom he is facing.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 6 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let your plans be dark and impenetrable as night, and when you move, fall like a thunderbolt.&#8221;</p><footer>The Art of War, chapter 7 &#183; tr. Lionel Giles, 1910 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/132/pg132-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
